'''October 76''' ({{Lang-bl|Octubre 76}}) refers to a period of {{W|civil unrest}} occurred throughout [[Balisca]], lasting some seven weeks and punctuated by demonstrations, general strikes, as well as the occupation of [[List of universities in Balisca|universities]] and {{W|Occupation of factories|factories}}. At the height of events the [[economy of Balisca]] came to a halt. The protests reached such a point that political leaders feared civil war or revolution. The protests inspired a generation of [[W:Protest art|protest art]] in the form of songs, imaginative graffiti, posters, and slogans, and is considered to have birthed the ''[[Desconexión]]'' movement.

The unrest began after the decision to postpone the [[1977 Baliscan confederal election|1976 elections]] for a year, with a series of [[W:Anti-authoritarianism|anti-authoritarian]] student occupation protests. It expanded in scope, rallying against increasing [[W:Elitism|elitism]] in traditional institutions, increasing [[W:Economic inequality|income disparities]] between urban and [[Rural society in Balisca|rural society]], and the increasing embrace of "[[W:Socialism from above|socialism from above]]" by the [[Revolutionary Front]]. Protests intensified after [[Secretary General of Balisca|Secretary General]] [[Mário Sá Justino]] forcefully condemned the protests as "unpatriotic" at a time of [[South Atlantic War|increased tensions with Argentina]], and called for the breaking up of the protests as part of the 1976 [[confederal emergency]] declaration. This led Balisca's [[Trade unions in Balisca|trade union confederations]] to call for {{W|sympathy strikes}}, which spread far more quickly than expected to involve 14 million workers, more than 16% of the total population of Balisca at the time, and the movement was quickly characterized by spontaneous and decentralized {{W|wildcat strike action|wildcat disposition}}.

The student occupations and general strikes initiated across Balisca were met with forceful attempts to quell them by university administrators, while confrontational rhetoric increased from members of the Revolutionary Front against the student protestors. This led to many street clashes between the opposing sides in several major cities across the Confederation, and sparked divisions within the [[Autonomous Militia|Autonomous Militias]] and the [[Confederal Congress]], complicating the confederal response to the Argentine invasion. 

